response:
Basically a loop is a set of instructions that execute repeatedly in a specific order until the end is reached. In C, there are two most basic types of loops:
 1. Do Loop that executes once.
 2. For Loop that executes a specified number of times. In either case, there are typically three elements to the loop: the initialization, the condition to check for (the last element of the loop), and the increment/decrement. 
Do loops are a single-directional type of loop; after the for loop is executed, the code on the surrounding block of code is executed again. Do loops (and also while loops) can get a bit confusing if the block of code is more than one line long. This is especially true with the double for loop. I would advise you to stop reading here. :) 

If you do more extensive programming, there are several more loops to learn (and more of them with nested loops) but for now my suggestion is to stick with the basics.

The basic loop functions are:

```
    void doSomething();
    {   // if condition is true

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

       if doSomething();

  }   // end of for loop
```

And here’s what an example of a do loop might look like:

```
    void doSomething(int a);
    {   // initialization and condition

       if a>10 {  // if condition is true

           a=15;

           doSomething();

       }  // condition can be any expression

       // more code

       if doMoreStuff();   // more statements for your doSomething

       // more code

    }   // end of do
```

You can also nest your if/else statement inside each other by putting every statement inside of a pair of curly curly braces:

```
  int i=0;
  for (int j=0; j<100; j++) {
    if (i%10==0) {
      i++;

      // more code

      if (doStuffIf(i>50){   // if condition is true

        // do stuff
      }
    }

    if (condition[i]){  // if condition is true, go to this statement

      // statement

    }
   }
``` 
The above statement is considered a for loop. The first part checks if the loop variable is divisible by 10, if true then it increases the loop counter and calls the statement if it is greater than the number 50. This continues until the loop counter reaches the number 100. Note that all statements before the loop and statements inside the loop can be completely different statements. Each statement within the conditional statements has to be separated by a space character.  

Another concept in C is the goto and the break statement. I would also suggest you look into the functions in the standard library, or more specifically the standard library in the case of the C programming language.

